      "text": "To determine the smallest positive multiple of 9 that is not a lucky integer, we need to check each multiple of 9 and its digit sum, and see if it is divisible by the sum of its digits. If not, we continue to the next multiple of 9.\n\nA lucky integer is defined as a number that is divisible by the sum of its digits. Let's start by examining the smallest multiples of 9 and their digit sums.\n\n1. Consider the number 9 itself: \n   - The digits of 9 are 9.\n   - The sum of the digits is \\(9\\).\n   - Since \\(9\\) divides \\(9\\), 9 is a lucky integer.\n\n2. Consider the next multiple of 9, which is 18:\n   - The digits of 18 are 1 and 8.\n   - The sum of the digits is \\(1 + 8 = 9\\).\n   - Since 18 is divisible by 9, 18 is a lucky integer.\n\n3. Consider the next multiple of 9, which is 27:\n   - The digits of 27 are 2 and 7.\n   - The sum of the digits is \\(2 + 7 = 9\\).\n   - Since 27 is divisible by 9, 27 is a lucky integer.\n\n4. Consider the next multiple of 9, which is 36:\n   - The digits of 36 are 3 and 6.\n   - The sum of the digits is \\(3 + 6 = 9\\).\n   - Since 36 is divisible by 9, 36 is a lucky integer.\n\n5. Consider the next multiple of 9, which is 45:\n   - The digits of 45 are 4 and 5.\n   - The sum of the digits is \\(4 + 5 = 9\\).\n   - Since 45 is divisible by 9, 45 is a lucky integer.\n\n6. Consider the next multiple of 9, which is 46:\n   - The digits of 46 are 4 and 6.\n   - The sum of the digits is \\(4 + 6 = 10\\).\n   - Since 46 is not divisible by 10 (and 10 does not divide 9), 46 is not a lucky integer.\n\nTherefore, the smallest positive multiple of 9 that is not a lucky integer is \\(\\boxed{46}\\).",
